1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,778
Pakistan, four thousand years ago.

2
00:00:02,778 --> 00:00:09,970
A planned city of around forty thousand people, with drains, wells, and bathrooms inside the houses.

3
00:00:09,970 --> 00:00:14,933
And then, according to the internet, it was erased by an atomic bomb.

4
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,359
The story has everything.

5
00:00:17,359 --> 00:00:20,965
Skeletons lying in the streets, holding hands.

6
00:00:20,965 --> 00:00:23,630
Bones as radioactive as Hiroshima.

7
00:00:23,630 --> 00:00:25,590
Bricks melted into glass.

8
00:00:25,590 --> 00:00:29,666
And an ancient Indian epic that describes the blast.

9
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:32,160
Tonight we do something simple.

10
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:40,399
We follow each claim upstream, one source at a time, the way a detective traces a rumour back to the first person who said it.

11
00:00:40,866 --> 00:00:41,471
Six hops.

12
00:00:41,471 --> 00:00:44,162
At every stop, the same three questions.

13
00:00:44,162 --> 00:00:44,969
Who said it?

14
00:00:44,969 --> 00:00:45,305
When?

15
00:00:45,305 --> 00:00:48,466
And what did they actually have in their hands?

16
00:00:48,933 --> 00:00:54,000
If the chain ends in a dig report or a laboratory, the claim stands.

17
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,099
If it ends in a book, a magazine, or a blank, it falls.

18
00:00:58,099 --> 00:00:59,366
Start at the top.

19
00:01:03,833 --> 00:01:04,478
Item one.

20
00:01:04,478 --> 00:01:06,628
The claim, as it stands today.

21
00:01:06,628 --> 00:01:13,866
It is repeated on hundreds of websites, in videos, in documentaries, always in nearly the same words.

22
00:01:14,333 --> 00:01:18,460
Skeletons in the street, unburied, many holding hands.

23
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:20,601
Fused glass at an epicentre.

24
00:01:20,601 --> 00:01:22,817
Radiation fifty times normal.

25
00:01:22,817 --> 00:01:27,633
And an epic poem, the Mahabharata, with a nuclear weapon in it.

26
00:01:28,099 --> 00:01:30,695
Notice what kind of evidence this is.

27
00:01:30,695 --> 00:01:34,764
Every item is a thing someone found, or measured, or read.

28
00:01:34,764 --> 00:01:37,500
So each one should leave a paper trail.

29
00:01:37,966 --> 00:01:41,470
Archaeology is boring in exactly the right way.

30
00:01:41,470 --> 00:01:46,391
Digs are published, finds are catalogued, labs sign their results.

31
00:01:46,391 --> 00:01:51,833
A real discovery of an ancient bomb site would leave a mountain of paper.

32
00:01:52,299 --> 00:01:54,866
So let's go and look for the paper.

33
00:01:54,866 --> 00:01:56,700
Hop one: the city itself.

34
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:58,900
What was Mohenjo-daro, really?

35
00:01:59,366 --> 00:01:59,981
Hop one.

36
00:01:59,981 --> 00:02:00,674
The city.

37
00:02:00,674 --> 00:02:09,211
Mohenjo-daro was built around twenty-five hundred B C, on a ridge beside the Indus River, in what is now Sindh.

38
00:02:09,211 --> 00:02:14,133
It was one of the great cities of the Indus Valley civilisation.

39
00:02:14,599 --> 00:02:15,844
Streets on a grid.

40
00:02:15,844 --> 00:02:17,366
Houses of fired brick.

41
00:02:17,366 --> 00:02:20,201
Covered drains running beneath the lanes.

42
00:02:20,201 --> 00:02:24,765
More than seven hundred wells, roughly one for every three houses.

43
00:02:24,765 --> 00:02:28,500
Nothing else in the world at that time looked like it.

44
00:02:28,966 --> 00:02:31,000
At its heart, the Great Bath.

45
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:37,311
Twelve metres long, seven wide, two and a half deep, lined with bitumen to hold the water.

46
00:02:37,311 --> 00:02:40,466
We still do not know exactly what it was for.

47
00:02:40,933 --> 00:02:45,271
The city stood on huge brick platforms, built against floods.

48
00:02:45,271 --> 00:02:49,182
One estimate puts that labour at four million man-days.

49
00:02:49,182 --> 00:02:53,733
This was a society that planned, organised, and fed its workers.

50
00:02:54,199 --> 00:02:55,522
No obvious palace.

51
00:02:55,522 --> 00:02:56,551
No royal tomb.

52
00:02:56,551 --> 00:02:58,167
No temple we can name.

53
00:02:58,167 --> 00:03:07,500
And one small bronze girl, hand on hip, with bangles up her arm, who tells us these people could cast metal and loved to dance.

54
00:03:07,966 --> 00:03:11,590
They also had a script, on thousands of tiny seals.

55
00:03:11,590 --> 00:03:12,939
Nobody can read it.

56
00:03:12,939 --> 00:03:20,400
That, and the silence about kings, is the real mystery of the Indus, and it has nothing to do with bombs.

57
00:03:20,866 --> 00:03:25,222
The city lay forgotten for about thirty-seven hundred years.

58
00:03:25,222 --> 00:03:30,086
An Indian archaeologist, R D Banerji, visited in nineteen nineteen.

59
00:03:30,086 --> 00:03:35,966
Large digs followed under Dikshit and John Marshall in the mid nineteen twenties.

60
00:03:36,433 --> 00:03:37,569
Notice something.

61
00:03:37,569 --> 00:03:42,182
Everything we know about the city comes from a century of excavation.

62
00:03:42,182 --> 00:03:46,929
So if something blew it up, this is where the first receipts should be.

63
00:03:46,929 --> 00:03:48,266
Hop two: the bodies.

64
00:03:48,733 --> 00:03:49,349
Hop two.

65
00:03:49,349 --> 00:03:50,427
The skeletons.

66
00:03:50,427 --> 00:03:56,665
Yes, human remains were found, scattered through the excavations at Mohenjo-daro.

67
00:03:56,665 --> 00:03:59,745
The count usually quoted is a few dozen.

68
00:03:59,745 --> 00:04:00,824
Not thousands.

69
00:04:00,824 --> 00:04:02,133
Not a whole city.

70
00:04:02,599 --> 00:04:09,150
The first big story came from the archaeologist Mortimer Wheeler, in the late nineteen forties.

71
00:04:09,150 --> 00:04:16,666
He saw those bones as victims of a massacre, killed in the last hours of the city by invaders from the north.

72
00:04:17,133 --> 00:04:22,421
Wheeler's invasion theory was dramatic, and it fit the thinking of his day.

73
00:04:22,421 --> 00:04:25,171
For a while it was the textbook answer.

74
00:04:25,171 --> 00:04:29,966
It also explains why people later believed in a sudden, violent end.

75
00:04:30,433 --> 00:04:37,858
Then, in nineteen sixty-four, the American archaeologist George Dales went back through the records.

76
00:04:37,858 --> 00:04:42,833
His paper has a blunt title: The Mythical Massacre at Mohenjo-daro.

77
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:51,532
Dales found that the skeletons came from different levels, which means different periods, scattered across the city's long history.

78
00:04:51,532 --> 00:04:53,166
They did not die together.

79
00:04:53,633 --> 00:04:56,096
Most showed no weapon injuries at all.

80
00:04:56,096 --> 00:05:01,411
And none lay in the citadel, the one place where a last stand would have happened.

81
00:05:01,411 --> 00:05:05,300
The massacre story collapsed, and archaeologists dropped it.

82
00:05:05,766 --> 00:05:08,255
What the diggers did find was mud.

83
00:05:08,255 --> 00:05:12,207
Layer on layer of silty clay, the signature of floods.

84
00:05:12,207 --> 00:05:19,966
The city was raised, rebuilt and flooded again, perhaps six times, as the Indus wandered across its plain.

85
00:05:20,433 --> 00:05:26,666
Flood and water-borne disease are a far more ordinary answer for a few unburied bodies.

86
00:05:26,666 --> 00:05:31,466
That is not proven either, but it matches the layers in the ground.

87
00:05:31,933 --> 00:05:36,776
So the skeletons trace back to a real dig, and a real correction.

88
00:05:36,776 --> 00:05:39,384
No blast, no hand-holding families.

89
00:05:39,384 --> 00:05:41,768
The first hop breaks on contact.

90
00:05:41,768 --> 00:05:43,333
Hop three: the glass.

91
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:44,479
Hop three.

92
00:05:44,479 --> 00:05:45,159
The glass.

93
00:05:45,159 --> 00:05:55,899
The nuclear story says there was an epicentre, about fifty yards wide, where everything was fused into crystal, and bricks melted on one side sixty yards out.

94
00:05:56,366 --> 00:05:59,742
Ask who said it, and the trail runs to a book.

95
00:05:59,742 --> 00:06:05,467
A British researcher, David Davenport, and an Italian writer, Ettore Vincenti.

96
00:06:05,467 --> 00:06:12,733
It came out in Italy in nineteen seventy-nine, with the title two thousand B C: Atomic Destruction.

97
00:06:13,199 --> 00:06:19,112
They reported temperatures near fifteen hundred degrees, followed by sudden cooling.

98
00:06:19,112 --> 00:06:20,309
A dramatic claim.

99
00:06:20,309 --> 00:06:24,251
So we look for the same thing in the excavation reports.

100
00:06:24,251 --> 00:06:25,800
And we find a silence.

101
00:06:26,266 --> 00:06:28,897
Now compare with a real atomic site.

102
00:06:28,897 --> 00:06:38,397
In nineteen forty-five, at the Trinity test in New Mexico, the blast turned desert sand into a thin green glass across the ground.

103
00:06:38,397 --> 00:06:41,466
A bomb leaves a signature you can pick up.

104
00:06:41,933 --> 00:06:44,033
Mohenjo-daro has no crater.

105
00:06:44,033 --> 00:06:45,199
No blast layer.

106
00:06:45,199 --> 00:06:47,144
No ring of melted ground.

107
00:06:47,144 --> 00:06:55,233
Not in Marshall's reports, not in Mackay's, not in Dales's, not in the surveys of the nineteen eighties.

108
00:06:55,699 --> 00:06:57,742
But there is glass of a kind.

109
00:06:57,742 --> 00:06:59,713
The city was a factory town.

110
00:06:59,713 --> 00:07:06,966
It had furnaces for copper and bronze, and kilns for baking bricks and pots, running hot for centuries.

111
00:07:07,433 --> 00:07:09,643
Overfired pots warp and glaze.

112
00:07:09,643 --> 00:07:12,295
Kiln waste melts into lumps of slag.

113
00:07:12,295 --> 00:07:16,716
A site full of kilns is going to produce fused, glassy bits.

114
00:07:16,716 --> 00:07:20,399
That is a boring answer, and it fits the evidence.

115
00:07:20,866 --> 00:07:23,129
There are a few traces of fire.

116
00:07:23,129 --> 00:07:27,218
One block of jewellers' workshops was apparently burned.

117
00:07:27,218 --> 00:07:34,300
But the layers show no wide ash, no charcoal horizon, no sign that the whole city burned at once.

118
00:07:34,766 --> 00:07:38,715
No published analysis ever matched the glass to a bomb.

119
00:07:38,715 --> 00:07:40,510
The trail ends at a book.

120
00:07:40,510 --> 00:07:42,233
Hop four: the radiation.

121
00:07:42,699 --> 00:07:43,399
Hop four.

122
00:07:43,399 --> 00:07:44,487
The radiation.

123
00:07:44,487 --> 00:07:53,347
The claim says a Soviet team found a skeleton with fifty times the natural level, matching Hiroshima and Nagasaki.

124
00:07:53,347 --> 00:07:54,590
Who measured it?

125
00:07:54,590 --> 00:07:56,766
That part is always missing.

126
00:07:57,233 --> 00:08:04,752
The usual source is a popular book called Riddles of Ancient History, by a Soviet writer, A Gorbovsky.

127
00:08:04,752 --> 00:08:06,669
It describes the skeleton.

128
00:08:06,669 --> 00:08:11,166
It does not give a sample number, a lab, a date, or a method.

129
00:08:11,633 --> 00:08:21,820
Researchers who have chased this specific claim say they cannot find any published laboratory measurement of elevated radiation at the site, ever.

130
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:22,866
Not one report.

131
00:08:23,333 --> 00:08:24,506
Now the physics.

132
00:08:24,506 --> 00:08:33,155
After a nuclear explosion, the dangerous isotopes that last longest are caesium one thirty-seven and strontium ninety.

133
00:08:33,155 --> 00:08:37,333
Their half-lives are about thirty and twenty-eight years.

134
00:08:37,799 --> 00:08:43,137
Wait thirty-eight hundred years, and you have more than a hundred half-lives.

135
00:08:43,137 --> 00:08:46,880
The radiation is gone, down to the natural background.

136
00:08:46,880 --> 00:08:50,000
Bones from the bronze age could not stay hot.

137
00:08:50,466 --> 00:08:52,521
Look at Hiroshima itself.

138
00:08:52,521 --> 00:08:54,494
Rebuilt, lived in, safe.

139
00:08:54,494 --> 00:08:59,427
No harmful radiation from nineteen forty-five remains there.

140
00:08:59,427 --> 00:09:05,100
Mohenjo-daro could not possibly be glowing after four thousand years.

141
00:09:05,566 --> 00:09:15,298
So the radioactive-skeleton claim is a measurement nobody can name, of a bone nobody can find, that physics says could not be true anyway.

142
00:09:15,298 --> 00:09:17,766
The trail dead-ends in a paperback.

143
00:09:18,233 --> 00:09:20,403
One hop left in the old books.

144
00:09:20,403 --> 00:09:23,658
The strongest piece of the story is the poem.

145
00:09:23,658 --> 00:09:25,033
Hop five: the epic.

146
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:26,165
Hop five.

147
00:09:26,165 --> 00:09:26,831
The epic.

148
00:09:26,831 --> 00:09:34,004
The Mahabharata is one of the longest poems ever written, a story of a great war between cousins.

149
00:09:34,004 --> 00:09:39,033
The nuclear story quotes it, and the quotes sound chillingly modern.

150
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:44,084
A single projectile charged with all the power of the universe.

151
00:09:44,084 --> 00:09:48,378
A column of smoke and flame as bright as ten thousand suns.

152
00:09:48,378 --> 00:09:49,761
Birds turned white.

153
00:09:49,761 --> 00:09:51,507
Hair and nails fell out.

154
00:09:51,507 --> 00:09:53,399
Where does this come from?

155
00:09:53,866 --> 00:09:56,161
The trail leads to the internet.

156
00:09:56,161 --> 00:10:05,198
In September two thousand, an article about ancient Indian atomic blasts appeared on an alternative-news site called KeelyNet.

157
00:10:05,198 --> 00:10:09,000
That is the earliest posting that anyone has located.

158
00:10:09,466 --> 00:10:16,253
Its source was listed as a magazine called World Island Review, January nineteen ninety-two.

159
00:10:16,253 --> 00:10:20,015
Brian Dunning of Skeptoid looked for that magazine.

160
00:10:20,015 --> 00:10:22,966
There is no record that it ever existed.

161
00:10:23,433 --> 00:10:27,534
The article quoted an archaeologist, Francis Taylor.

162
00:10:27,534 --> 00:10:33,212
Nobody has found any archaeology paper published by anyone of that name.

163
00:10:33,212 --> 00:10:36,366
It also quoted a historian, K M Ganguli.

164
00:10:36,833 --> 00:10:39,113
Kisari Mohan Ganguli was real.

165
00:10:39,113 --> 00:10:46,484
He translated the Mahabharata into English in the eighteen eighties, long before the atomic bomb.

166
00:10:46,484 --> 00:10:51,500
Nowhere is there a record that he wrote the lines credited to him.

167
00:10:51,966 --> 00:10:56,238
Dunning also searched the full translation for the famous quotes.

168
00:10:56,238 --> 00:10:57,750
He could not find them.

169
00:10:57,750 --> 00:10:58,933
Nothing like them.

170
00:10:58,933 --> 00:11:03,600
The passages seem to have been invented, then copied from site to site.

171
00:11:04,066 --> 00:11:08,682
He noticed the line about warriors swept away like dry leaves.

172
00:11:08,682 --> 00:11:14,787
It sounds a lot like a famous scene in a nineteen ninety-one science fiction film.

173
00:11:14,787 --> 00:11:16,127
Maybe coincidence.

174
00:11:16,127 --> 00:11:18,733
Maybe a writer borrowing a picture.

175
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:22,030
Even the Oppenheimer line is slippery.

176
00:11:22,030 --> 00:11:27,394
Robert Oppenheimer quoted the Gita on television in nineteen sixty-five.

177
00:11:27,394 --> 00:11:34,172
The tale that he told students in nineteen fifty-two that an earlier atom bomb had existed?

178
00:11:34,172 --> 00:11:35,066
No evidence.

179
00:11:35,533 --> 00:11:36,146
Hop six.

180
00:11:36,146 --> 00:11:37,373
The real ending.

181
00:11:37,373 --> 00:11:40,746
If it was not a bomb, what emptied the city?

182
00:11:40,746 --> 00:11:49,333
A team led by Liviu Giosan of Woods Hole studied the rivers, and published in the journal PNAS in twenty twelve.

183
00:11:49,799 --> 00:11:53,911
They found that the monsoon had been weakening for centuries.

184
00:11:53,911 --> 00:12:02,066
By around four thousand years ago, the rains that fed the Indus rivers and the Ghaggar-Hakra system were at their lowest.

185
00:12:02,533 --> 00:12:06,553
Indus farming depended on yearly floods, not on canals.

186
00:12:06,553 --> 00:12:09,842
As the floods weakened, harvests got riskier.

187
00:12:09,842 --> 00:12:16,566
Cities that lived on surplus food began to shrink, and people moved east toward wetter land.

188
00:12:17,033 --> 00:12:26,174
Their own words: a gradual decline in flood intensity, and a slow settlement shift eastwards, rather than a sudden collapse.

189
00:12:26,174 --> 00:12:27,206
No single day.

190
00:12:27,206 --> 00:12:28,091
No fireball.

191
00:12:28,091 --> 00:12:30,966
A civilisation that slowly walked away.

192
00:12:31,433 --> 00:12:33,038
The chain is complete.

193
00:12:33,038 --> 00:12:37,854
Every hop that touches real research points to climate and rivers.

194
00:12:37,854 --> 00:12:42,159
Every hop that points to a bomb ends in a book, or a blank.

195
00:12:42,159 --> 00:12:43,399
Time to score it.

196
00:12:43,866 --> 00:12:44,677
Case file.

197
00:12:44,677 --> 00:12:46,866
Time to score Mohenjo-daro.

198
00:12:47,333 --> 00:12:50,969
A planned city of about forty thousand: evidence.

199
00:12:50,969 --> 00:12:56,609
A slow decline between nineteen hundred and seventeen hundred B C: evidence.

200
00:12:56,609 --> 00:13:01,656
A few dozen skeletons from many periods, mostly unwounded: evidence.

201
00:13:01,656 --> 00:13:05,366
A weakening monsoon and shifting rivers: evidence.

202
00:13:05,833 --> 00:13:09,599
A vitrified epicentre: kiln waste, never analysed.

203
00:13:09,599 --> 00:13:14,118
Radioactive bones: no measurement, and physics rules it out.

204
00:13:14,118 --> 00:13:19,766
Mahabharata nuclear quotes: not in the text, traced to an invented article.

205
00:13:20,233 --> 00:13:21,121
The verdict.

206
00:13:21,121 --> 00:13:21,490
Myth.

207
00:13:21,490 --> 00:13:24,079
No crater, no burn layer, no blast.

208
00:13:24,079 --> 00:13:32,438
The ancient nuclear story began as a book in nineteen seventy-nine and became an internet legend in two thousand.

209
00:13:32,438 --> 00:13:36,433
The city itself ended quietly, with a failing monsoon.

210
00:13:36,899 --> 00:13:40,583
And here is the part that no fireball story can match.

211
00:13:40,583 --> 00:13:43,721
The real city is still there, and it is dying.

212
00:13:43,721 --> 00:13:51,566
Salt in the groundwater is eating its bricks, and in twenty twelve experts warned it could vanish by twenty thirty.

213
00:13:52,033 --> 00:13:54,582
The truth is more precious than the myth.

214
00:13:54,582 --> 00:14:02,166
A people who built drains and wells and a great bath four and a half thousand years ago deserve to be remembered for that.

215
00:14:02,633 --> 00:14:05,604
Next case: March nineteen ninety-seven.

216
00:14:05,604 --> 00:14:10,938
Thousands of people in Arizona see a vast, silent formation of lights.

217
00:14:10,938 --> 00:14:13,985
A mile-wide craft, or flares and planes?

218
00:14:13,985 --> 00:14:14,900
We trace it.

219
00:14:15,366 --> 00:14:18,705
Did you ever see the Mohenjo-daro nuclear story?

220
00:14:18,705 --> 00:14:24,966
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery, traced to its source.
